<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/glossary#demotic-egyptian-script">Glossary</a> · Term · script</p><h1 class="enc-title">Demotic Egyptian script</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 650 BCE–<a href="/years/452">452 CE</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/7th-century-bce">7th century BCE</a> · Also known as Demotic, Enchorial Egyptian</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Demotic is the ancient Egyptian script derived from northern forms of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hieratic">hieratic</a> used in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nile-delta">Nile Delta</a>, named 'popular' by the Greek historian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herodotus">Herodotus</a>; the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egyptians">Egyptians</a> called it 'document writing'. Written from right to left, it developed in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lower-egypt">Lower Egypt</a> late in the Twenty-fifth Dynasty and became the official administrative and legal script under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amasis-ii">Amasis II</a>. Used first for administrative, legal and commercial texts, in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ptolemaic-kingdom">Ptolemaic Kingdom</a> it also served literature and religion, then declined in public life under Roman rule. The last dated example is a graffito at the temple of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/isis">Isis</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philae">Philae</a> from 452 CE. Its 32 lines on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rosetta-stone">Rosetta Stone</a> were deciphered before the hieroglyphs.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/demotic-egyptian-script.jpg" alt="Demotic Egyptian script" width="330" height="224" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Chris 73 · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:DemoticScriptsRosettaStoneReplica.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>Demotic derives from northern forms of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hieratic">hieratic</a> used in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nile-delta">Nile Delta</a>. Egyptians called it document writing, and the 2nd-century scholar <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/clement-of-alexandria">Clement of Alexandria</a> called it letter-writing. Early Demotic developed in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lower-egypt">Lower Egypt</a> during the later part of the Twenty-fifth Dynasty and is found especially on steles from the Serapeum of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saqqara">Saqqara</a>. It is generally dated between 650 and 400 BCE, since most of its texts belong to the Twenty-sixth Dynasty and to the following Achaemenid rule. After the reunification of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/psamtik-i">Psamtik I</a>, Demotic replaced Abnormal Hieratic in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/upper-egypt">Upper Egypt</a>, and under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amasis-ii">Amasis II</a> it became the official administrative and legal script. It is written and read from right to left.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">How it was used</h2><p>At first Demotic served only administrative, legal and commercial texts, while hieroglyphs and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hieratic">hieratic</a> were kept for religious texts and literature. Middle Demotic, from about 400 to 30 BCE, was the script of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ptolemaic-kingdom">Ptolemaic Kingdom</a>, and from the 4th century BCE it rose in status, as its growing use for literary and religious texts shows. By the end of the 3rd century BCE Koine Greek was the administrative language, and Demotic contracts lost most of their legal force unless they carried a note in Greek of being registered with the authorities. Alphabetic or uniliteral signs make up between a third and a half of all signs in a given text, and foreign words are written almost entirely with them.</p><p>Under Roman rule Demotic was used less and less in public life, though literary texts in Late Demotic survive, especially from the 1st and 2nd centuries CE. Greek did not replace Egyptian as the language of everyday speech, but Demotic was afterwards used only for a few ostraca, subscriptions to Greek texts, mummy labels and graffiti. The last dated example is a graffito on the walls of the temple of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/isis">Isis</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philae">Philae</a>, dated 12 December 452, which reads simply Petise, son of Petosiris.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Discovery and study</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rosetta-stone">Rosetta Stone</a>, discovered in 1799, is inscribed with a proclamation in three scripts: hieroglyphs, Demotic and Greek. It has 32 lines of Demotic, the middle script. The Demotic was deciphered before the hieroglyphs, beginning with the efforts of Antoine Isaac Silvestre de Sacy. Early Western scholars, notably <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thomas-young-scientist">Thomas Young</a>, called it Enchorial Egyptian.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Demotic Egyptian script” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Coptic, Egyptian written in Greek letters with several signs taken from <b>Demotic</b>, became the language of a new literature and is still used in the liturgy of the Coptic Orthodox Church.</blockquote><a href="/empire/roman-egypt">Roman Egypt · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>…about 2700 to 2000 BCE; Middle Egyptian, the classical language of the Middle Kingdom, used for inscriptions until the end of antiquity; Late Egyptian, the language of the New Kingdom from about 1350 BCE; <b>Demotic</b>, from the 7th century BCE; and Coptic, written from the 3rd century CE.</blockquote><a href="/language/ancient-egyptian">Ancient Egyptian · Origins</a></li><li><blockquote>It belongs to the Later Egyptian phase, the colloquial speech of the later periods, and its core vocabulary is closest to the <b>Demotic</b> phase before it.</blockquote><a href="/language/coptic">Coptic · Origins</a></li><li><blockquote>The Greek and hieroglyphic texts also give a second date, 27 November 197 BCE, the anniversary of the coronation, which the <b>Demotic</b> text contradicts.</blockquote><a href="/work/rosetta-stone">Rosetta Stone · What It Is</a></li><li><blockquote>The kings called the priests of all Egypt to synods, at Canopus in 238 BCE and at Memphis in 217 BCE; the decree of 217 BCE, in hieroglyphs, <b>Demotic</b> and Greek, presented the king as Horus and set up his statue in every temple.</blockquote><a href="/societies/ptolemaic-egypt">Social Hierarchy of Ptolemaic Egypt</a></li><li><blockquote>Art and texts deliberately revived Old Kingdom models, and the <b>Demotic</b> script came into use.</blockquote><a href="/state/twenty-sixth-dynasty-of-egypt">Twenty-sixth Dynasty of Egypt</a></li></ul></section>
